<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siloam tunnel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 700 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> · Also known as Hezekiah's tunnel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Siloam tunnel, also called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hezekiah">Hezekiah</a>'s tunnel, is an ancient water channel about 533 metres long cut through the rock beneath the City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. It carries water from the Gihon spring to the Pool of Siloam and is usually dated to King Hezekiah's preparations for the Assyrian siege of 701 BCE. An inscription found in it in 1880 describes the two teams of diggers meeting.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siloam-tunnel.jpg" alt="Siloam tunnel" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tamar Hayardeni תמר הירדני · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siloam22.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
